win32u: OpenGL in windows through the surfaceless EGL driver (no Wayland EGL on Haiku)

Mesa on Haiku has no Wayland EGL platform, so Wine's OpenGL uses win32u's own
EGL driver on the surfaceless platform. Its window drawable (an emulated
framebuffer) had two gaps for the Raspberry Pi 4 with Mesa's V3D, an OpenGL 2.1
driver: the framebuffer's color textures were attached with glFramebufferTexture
(OpenGL 3.2), which an OpenGL 2.1 context does not have, so the framebuffer was
incomplete; and with no driver drawable to present to, nothing showed a finished
frame in the window. The textures are now attached with glFramebufferTexture2D
(OpenGL 3.0 / ARB_framebuffer_object), and wglSwapBuffers (or a flush that
opengl32 marks as presenting the front buffer) reads the front buffer back and
draws it as a DIB into the application's DC (wglSwapBuffers', or the thread's
current draw DC) — a DC fetched in the swap path did not reach the window.

Wine 11.20 already makes the framebuffer with the OpenGL 3.0 calls instead of
direct state access, in its own internal context (so no caller bindings to keep),
loads every GL entry point, and sets GL's default draw buffer (the back one of a
double-buffered window) when a context is made current: those parts of the Wine
11.0 patch are gone. A swap exchanges the front and back textures, so the
finished frame is attachment 0 after it.

--- a/dlls/win32u/opengl.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/opengl.c
@@ -855,6 +855,8 @@
